### Iter 162 — BWD_SUB + BWD_DEMOD isolated +31%

Iter 160 had tried fwd_sub + bwd_sub + bwd_demod TOGETHER and observed
a -7% regression on AndAssoc, dismissed the trio.  Iter 162 split them
and found:

  - FWD_SUB alone: -17% (regresses to 192 rules)
  - BWD_SUB alone: +30% (232 -> 301 rules)
  - BWD_DEMOD alone: noise (218 vs 217)
  - BWD_SUB + BWD_DEMOD: +31% (232 -> 304 rules)
  - All three: -7% (FWD_SUB pulls the trio down)

Forward subsumption costs more in subsumption checks than it saves in
rule drops on AndAssoc.  Backward subsumption pays off because fewer
surviving rules => smaller discrimination tree => cheaper unorient
queries every step.  Backward demodulation adds another +1% layered
on top.

Lesson: when a multi-flag combo regresses, do the per-flag isolation
before declaring the bundle dead.  The trio's "regression" hid a
real +31% lever inside.

State: 304 rules / 30s sustained.  Cumulative honest improvement vs
iter-136 baseline (195 rules / 30s): +56%.

Also wired THVM_ATP_FWD_SUB / BWD_SUB / BWD_DEMOD as independent env
toggles so future A/B work doesn't need to edit the preset.

