### Iter 157 — CRITICAL fix + honest baseline

Re-ran test_kbo (which I'd been skipping after most commits, only
checking test_atp + test_lpo).  Found 17/196 failures in
"lpo/pretest-agrees-with-full-recursion".  Traced to iter-138's
flatterm port: an over-eager `if (a[pa].sz != b[pb].sz) return LPO_UN;`
check before lex compare.  KboFlatNode.sz is the subtree-span node
count, NOT arity -- two same-head terms with different child shapes
can have different sz (e.g. f(x,y) vs f(g(x),y)).  The check silently
returned UN when correct verdict was GT/LT.  All iters 138-156
inherited it.

**Honest impact**: AndAssoc bench drops from 315 -> 217 rules / 30s
after the fix.  Sound baseline vs iter-136 (195 rules / 30s on
Term-tree lpo_rec): real +10-11% throughput, NOT the previously
reported +60%.

Why ATP tests stayed green: bug only fires on equal-head,
different-child-shape pairs that don't arise in the Sheffer test
suite (uniform arity, tight shape constraints).  In completion, the
wrong UN return made the engine think rules were unorientable -- the
engine handled that gracefully (the rule went into the unorient
queue) but the bench's rules/30s metric was inflated because
"unorientable" rule-paths skipped some work that the orient-and-
install path would have done.

Lesson: ALWAYS run test_kbo + test_lpo + test_atp + WL paclet
test after each LPO/KBO change.  test_atp alone is insufficient
coverage for soundness regressions.

### Iter 156 — heap_read tightening sweep across all ATP recursive walks

Applied the iter-155 lpo_flat_encode_rec pattern (direct heap_read for
arity + children, bypasses term_ctr_at's redundant arity refetch) to
the remaining ATP Term-walking helpers:

  - thvm_unify_apply (unify/_.c)
  - thvm_subst_apply (rewrite/_.c)
  - thvm_match (rewrite/_.c -- hot in atp_ft_unorient_step candidate loop)
  - thvm_rename_vars (unify/_.c -- per-CP rule rename)
  - thvm_unify (unify/_.c -- CP-gen unification of two rule LHSs)
  - unify_occurs (unify/_.c -- occurs check during unification)
  - nv_rewrite (unify/_.c -- thvm_normalize_vars second pass)

AndAssoc within noise (315-316 vs 315 prior) since these helpers
collectively account for <5% of runtime; commit for the per-call
cycle savings.  Together with the iter-155 LPO encoder, all ATP-side
recursive Term walks now use direct heap_read instead of going through
term_ctr_at's internal term_ctr_n refetch.

### Iter 155 — LPO-specific encoder skips term_ctr_at's redundant heap_read

| Commit                                            | Rules | Wall | Delta |
|---------------------------------------------------|-------|------|-------|
| iter-153 final                                    | 308-313| 30s |  --   |
| local LPO encoder, direct heap_read for children  |  315  | 30s  | +1.5% |
| branch hints on cold paths                        |  315  | 30s  | noise |

The encoder win: kbo_flat_encode called term_ctr_at per child, which
internally re-reads the arity cell via term_ctr_n -- an extra heap_read
per iteration.  Local lpo_flat_encode_rec caches base = term_val(t) once
and reads each child via heap_read(base + 1 + i) directly.

Also stores precedence into .w via cfg->precedence[lab] directly
(was going through kbo_flat_encode's stub cfg with weights=precedence
indirection).

AndAssoc bench: 315 rules / 30s sustained across 3 runs (was 308-313
noise band on the kbo_flat_encode path).  Cumulative ~+60% throughput
from iter-136 baseline.

