at entry).  UNSOUND for repeat-occurrence variables: pattern f(X, X)
matched against f(A, B) would return 1 on the inner X match instead of
failing the kbo_eq(A, B) check.  Reverted.

State holds at 309-311 rules / 30s.

### Iter 153 — non-LPO skip-unchanged in rename_vars + nv_rewrite

Continued the iter-152 hash-cons skip-unchanged sweep across the rest
of the ATP code's term-rebuilding helpers:

  - thvm_rename_vars: identity-rename short-circuit (offset==0); also
    skip-unchanged in CTR (closed-term ground goal sides).
  - nv_rewrite (thvm_normalize_vars second pass): identity-dense skip
    (FVR's old id == its dense position) + skip-unchanged in CTR.

Verified the remaining `term_new_ctr` sites:
- thvm_rewrite_step, atp_ic_rewrite_step, atp_ri_build, atp_norm_memo
  all already had the skip-unchanged pattern.
- thvm_materialize, realize_many wnf rebuilds are JIT/schedule path,
  not ATP.

AndAssoc bench within noise (310-312 vs 308-313 prior).  These don't
shift the AndAssoc rules/30s needle (the recursive walks are <1.5% of
runtime each) but the savings hold for any ATP workload whose subst,
unify, or alpha-renaming binds sparse.

### Iter 152 — non-LPO unify/subst skip-unchanged

Attacked the non-LPO machinery for the first time.  Two general-purpose
correctness opts:

  - thvm_unify_apply: tracks whether ANY subterm rewrote during the
    recursive descent.  If not, returns the original t pointer instead
    of allocating a fresh term_new_ctr.  Used during CP-gen rule rename.
  - thvm_subst_apply: same hash-cons skip pattern.  Used during
    atp_ft_unorient_step's candidate loop (subst applied to rule RHS
    to get `repl`).

AndAssoc bench within noise (308-310 vs 308-311).  These don't shift
the rules/30s needle on this workload (per-allocation saving is small
relative to total runtime) but they hold true for any CP-gen workload
that touches subst with sparse bindings.

Also tried and reverted: packed (key, epoch_cmp) memo entries -- two
32-bit fields instead of (pa, pb, epoch, cmp, side) struct.  Hoped to
collapse the memo compare to one 64-bit read; in practice the compiler
optimizes the original struct equally well.

### Iter 151 — Flatrec default-on

Iters 137-149's stacked LPO machinery (per-level Vortest + direct
subterm scan + flatterm recursive LPO body + AlleDiff post-lex skip +
inline helpers + branch hints + WM precedence dispatch) measured +60%
throughput with zero regressions across test_lpo (12/12), test_atp
(135624/135624), and the WL paclet ATP test suite.

Promoted the gate from opt-in (THVM_LPO_FLAT_REC=1) to default-on
(THVM_LPO_FLAT_REC=0 disables).  All future thvm_lpo callers get the
optimized path by default -- atp_compare in the rewrite/normalize/CP-
gen loops automatically benefits.

AndAssoc bench with no env knob: 308-311 rules / 30s (was 195-198 on
the Term-tree fallback path).  +57% baseline.

Also tried: GROUND_JOIN, CONNECT, MNF, RIGHT_REDUCE env flags
(WALDMEISTER preset already chooses the optimal combination); CP
weight modes 0-7 (Mix=4 is optimal at 311 vs Mix2=5 at 307; everything
else is slower).  No further configuration win available.

### Iter 150 — no successful opts

Tried 3 micro-opts, all reverted:
- Fibonacci-multiplier hash (-2.5% rules; 3-multiply xor hash spreads
  better even though it has more ops)
- Drop dead lex == LPO_EQ check (-1.6%; compiler benefits from the
  defensive check as a branch hint structure)
- __builtin_expect(lex == LPO_GT, 1) (-1.6%; gcc default schedule beats
  it for this workload)

State: 315 rules / 30s sustained.  Direct LPO compare optimization is
at structural floor for this workload -- further wins must come from
patterns I haven't spotted yet in WM source OR from non-LPO machinery
(thvm_unify_apply 81 samples = 1.2% of runtime, atp_dt_descend ~ same).

### Iter 149 — WM's LPOGroesserAlleDiff (skip post-lex EQ children)

| Commit                                            | Rules | Wall | Delta |
|---------------------------------------------------|-------|------|-------|
| iter-148 final                                    |  310-313| 30s |  --   |
| drop __builtin_expect on slice_eq                 |  312-314| 30s | noise+|
| port WM AlleDiff -- skip post-lex EQ children     |  315  | 30s  | +1.0% |
| apply AlleDiff to LT direction too                |  315  | 30s  | noise |

Cumulative iter-149: +1% from AlleDiff (real algorithmic win, not micro-
opt).  Re-reading WM's LP_LPO source uncovered that after lex compare
finds the differing position, earlier children are pairwise EQ -- so
dominates_all_args trivially holds on them via case (1) (some_arg_dom
fires with the matching equal-child).  WM's LPOGroesserAlleDiff returns
the diff position; subsequent LPOGroesserEx uses TO_NaechsterTeilterm(Diff)
to start AFTER the differing child.

Port: lpo_flat_lex_diff returns the (ca, cb) at the differing position;
lpo_flat_dominates_from(start_child=diff_pos) skips earlier checks.

This is the third major pattern unlocked by careful WM source reading
(after per-level Vortest in iter-145 and direct subterm scan in iter-146).
The lesson holds: WM source is dense and each careful pass surfaces a
new optimization.

State: 315 rules / 30s sustained on AndAssoc LPO+Mix+Wald+Flatrec.
Cumulative throughput improvement from iter-136 baseline: +60% (197 ->
315 rules / 30s).

### Iter 148 — cold-mark unequal-precedence branches

For Sheffer (single internal symbol nand) and most equational theories
with one internal function symbol, the recursive lpo_flat_rec_compute
calls almost always see prec_a == prec_b -- only at leaf positions
involving constants does unequal precedence arise.

Tagging both unequal-prec branches as cold via __builtin_expect lets
gcc lay the equal-prec fall-through as straight-line code with no
taken branch on the hot path.

AndAssoc LPO+Mix+Wald+Flatrec: 315 rules / 30s (was 310, noise ±3).
+1.6%.  Cumulative ~60% closure of the 24x per-rule gap to wmcli.

Tried and reverted: hints on the equal-prec case (1) some_arg_dom
calls (they fire OFTEN during completion -- my hint was wrong-direction),
and on lex == LPO_EQ (path is dead-code reachable but rarely fires
in practice -- noise rather than win).

### Iter 147 — drop redundant lpo_eq + arity-2 lex unroll

| Commit                                            | Rules | Wall | Delta |
|---------------------------------------------------|-------|------|-------|
| iter-146 final                                    |  307  | 30s  |  --   |
| drop top-level lpo_eq before flatrec              |  307  | 30s  | noise |
| unroll lex compare's arity-2 fast path            |  310  | 30s  | +1.0% |

Cumulative iter-147: +1.0% from the lex unroll.  Total closure 57.4%.

The lpo_eq removal was a code-cleanup (was running a recursive Term-tree
walk redundantly before slice_eq inside lpo_flat_rec_compute).  The lex
unroll's win comes from exposing the loop body as straight-line code so
gcc can interleave the two memo-wrapped lpo_flat_rec calls with no
branch in between -- per-iteration cost shrinks below what the loop
counter increment costs.

Tried and reverted: always_inline lpo_flat_rec wrapper (recursive
function, compiler bails), unroll some_arg_dom + dominates_all_args
(no measurable gain on top of compile-driven inlining).

### Iter 146 — direct subterm scan + branch hints

| Commit                                            | Rules | Wall | Delta |
|---------------------------------------------------|-------|------|-------|
| iter-145 final                                    |  291  | 30s  |  --   |
| replace candidate-list with direct subterm scan   |  307  | 30s  | +5.5% |
| __builtin_expect on slice_eq + var-path           |  310* | 30s  | +1.0% |

(* noise band ±2, sustained ~307 rules/30s.)

Cumulative iter-146: +5.5% over iter-145.  Total closure 55.8% from
the matched-algorithm baseline.

Tried and reverted: single-direction Vortest by size (noise), un-inline
some_arg_dom var check (compiler already optimized), builtin_expect in
Vortest scan (wrong direction -- nand-sym matches are common, not rare),
local LPO-specific encoder (compiler already inlined kbo_flat_encode),
noinline cold-var-path extraction (var checks too cheap to matter).

The direct subterm scan was the big iter-146 win: WM's Termliste
candidate-list approach has O(na * avg_nk) cost, but for Sheffer where
many positions have b[0].sym = nand the candidates pile up to nk ~ na/2.
A direct "for each starting position, check if slice matches" loop is
O((na-nb)*nb) with the sz-mismatch fast-fail killing most starting
positions before the inner compare.

### Iter 145 — per-level Vortest unlocks +16%

The big iter-142 conclusion ("LPO compare at structural floor") was
wrong.  Re-reading WM's LP_LPOGroesser uncovered a missed pattern: WM
runs LV_VortestLPOGroesser at EVERY recursion entry, catching strict-
subterm cases without paying the full case-(1) some_arg_dominates +
recursive descent at that level.  My Vortest was only at the top of
thvm_lpo, so deeper levels were re-doing all the case-(1) work.

Four iter-145 commits stacking on AndAssoc LPO+Mix+Wald+Flatrec:

| Commit                                            | Rules | Wall | Delta |
|---------------------------------------------------|-------|------|-------|
| iter-143 baseline                                 |  259  | 30s  |  --   |
| per-level Vortest in lpo_flat_rec_compute         |  264  | 30s  | +1.9% |
| fast-fail Vortest when na <= nb                   |  277  | 30s  | +4.9% |
| precedence dispatch before case (1) (WM order)    |  279  | 30s  | +0.7% |
| always_inline lpo_pretest_groesser_flat           |  291  | 30s  | +4.3% |

Cumulative iter-145: +12.4% over iter-143; 47.7% closure of the 24x
gap to wmcli's 161 r/s.  Tests 135624/135624 + LPO 12/12 stable.

The fast-fail (`na <= nb` rules out b-strict-subterm-of-a in O(1))
was the biggest single win.  The always_inline was second -- without
it gcc emitted lpo_pretest_groesser_flat as a frame-setup callee at
every recursion level.

Profile after stack:
  lpo_flat_rec_compute (entry)              624 samples
  lpo_flat_rec_compute (+1456 mid-body)     310
  lpo_flat_rec_compute (+1820)              284
  lpo_flat_rec wrapper (memo path)          155
  lpo_flat_rec_compute (+3684 deep)         117
  thvm_unify_apply                           61

Compute is now a single ~4KB function with all helpers inlined; the
recursive descent shows as offsets into ONE function body rather than
a fan-out of separate callees.  I-cache pressure is a concern but
un-inlining lex regressed (-1% rules) so the locality win outweighs
the size cost.

Tried but reverted: simpler memo hash (more collisions, -6.5%),
__attribute__((hot)) on compute (compiler picks worse schedule,
-2.3%), cross-call encode cache (no hit on Sheffer workload),
memcmp slice_eq (call overhead beats loop for tiny slices), un-
inline lex (-1%).

### Iter 143 — top-level memo skip

The top-level `lpo_flat_rec(g_lpo_flat_a, 0, g_lpo_flat_b, 0)` always
hashed to memo slot 0, so every thvm_lpo call paid full memo lookup
overhead on a single hot slot that the recursive descent kept
overwriting.  Bypass it -- call lpo_flat_rec_compute directly at the
top -- and let only the recursive children memoize.

AndAssoc LPO+Mix+Wald+Flatrec: 259 rules / 30s (was 253).  +2.4%.
Cumulative 31.5% closure of the 24x gap.  Profile post-skip:
lpo_flat_rec_compute +0 dropped from 1014 -> 943 samples; the
lpo_flat_rec wrapper +0 dropped from 1014 -> 85.  The freed cycles
went into raising rule throughput.

Also forced always_inline on lpo_flat_lex for consistency with the
other inlined helpers (no measurable AndAssoc impact).

Profile-after-optimization (atp_ft_unorient_step subtree, 8s sample):
  atp_ft_unorient_step @+936         3003  (82% of unorient step)
  → atp_compare @+1972               2467
    → lpo_flat_rec @+564             1341  (memo store + return)
      → lpo_flat_rec @+480 (recur)    998
        → lpo_flat_rec @+480           541
          → ...                       330 → 173 → 81 → 37

The recursive descent IS where the cost lives.  Memo lookup is small per
call; the bulk is in the inlined slice_eq + some_arg_dominates +
dominates_all_args body.  At this point per-call cost is structural to
the algorithm; closing the remaining ~19x to WM's 161 r/s requires:

  - thvm_subst_apply / thvm_match port: the per-candidate match before
    the LPO compare.
  - atp_dt_descend_rec port: discrim-tree descent for index queries.
  - atp_ri_descend_rec port: rule-index linear scan when the DT misses.

WM's MO_RegelGefunden (rule install, 1352 WM-samples) has no thvm hot
analog -- the install path is structurally different and may not be the
bottleneck thvm-side.  The right next attack is the per-CP-evaluation
chain (thvm_match -> thvm_subst_apply -> atp_compare), which dominates
the inner loop.

================================================================
iter 178 -- BWD_SUB SEGV fix + CP cap-lift (2x rules / 30s)
================================================================

Multi-agent workflow ("thvm-andassoc-gap-attack") spawned 4 parallel
investigation agents (profile-cp-step, compare-cp-selection,
audit-cp-enumeration, audit-per-rule-cost) and 1 synthesis agent.
The synthesis picked AUDIT-CP-ENUMERATION's findings as highest
leverage: silent CP loss at CP_MAX_DEPTH=8 + ATP_CP_BATCH=1024.

DISCOVERED A PRE-EXISTING SEGV on HEAD: bench WM-preset crashed at
1.3s into AndAssoc.  Bisected to BWD_SUB; root cause was a NULL
g_atp_ri_rhs in atp_unorient_step_indexed (the non-flatterm path):
leaf_collect_unorient dereferences g_atp_ri_rhs[rule] for r->l-face
records but only the flatterm-mixed path ever set the global.
Compounded by atp_ri_rebuild indexing dead-sentinel rules.

Fix lands in commit 7e3fdab9:
  - atp_ri_rebuild skips s->r_dead[i] in BOTH rule_index AND
    unorient_index passes.
  - atp_unorient_step_indexed sets g_atp_ri_rhs = s->rhs before
    descend; matches the flatterm-mixed path.
  - CP_MAX_DEPTH 8 -> 16; ATP_CP_BATCH 1024 -> 4096.
  - thvm_cp_caps_reset/get exposed; bench prints cap_hits +
    depth_capped_positions at saturation end.

MEASUREMENTS (WALDMEISTER preset, AndAssoc, 30s wall):

  state                   rules    cps     caps                  notes
  HEAD (pre-fix)            ~61   ~270    --                    SEGV @ 1.3s
  SEGV fix only            197   8869   depth_capped=4269       runs cleanly
  + cap-lift to 16/4096    421   47796  depth_capped=0          repro 2x

Regression scan (other goals, 15s, WM preset):
  wolfram   356 rules  caps: 0 / 0
  mccune    467 rules  caps: 0 / 0
  robbins   3520 rules caps: 0 / 0

Surgical tests all green:
  test_atp 135624/135624, test_kbo 204/204, test_lpo 12/12.

PHASE PROFILE @ 30s (cap-lifted):
  cp-gen        13.03s  (43%)
  push-norm     10.05s  (33%)
  interreduce    3.97s  (13%)
  unorient-step  8.83s  total, 5M calls, 88% wasted (no fire)
  unf-memo       76% hit-ratio
  push-time full-R normalizes: 1.45M  (lazy_normalize=1 but huge)

NEXT LEVERAGE TARGETS (workflow findings still standing):
  1. LPO compare cascade in lpo_flat_rec_compute (the 32% from
     iter 177 profile) -- memo cache misses on fresh CP normalisations.
  2. atp_dt_descend_rec / atp_ri_descend_rec retrieval (~21%) -- STAR
     backtracking memcmp on repeated variable patterns.
  3. push-time normalizations: 1.45M is suspiciously high for
     lazy_normalize=1; the lazy flag should defer most of these.
  4. unorient-step 88% wasted (post-memo): index is too coarse;
     candidates that fail KBO_GT after match.

The CP-throughput gap to WM (440K CPs/sec) is still ~30x at our
new 421 rules/30s but the SEGV-blocked baseline has been unblocked.
Workflow-driven iteration is now the productive cadence.

