================================================================
iter 181 -- WM PARITY ACHIEVED on AndAssoc (engine default!)
================================================================

KEY FINDING:  THE BARE ENGINE (no WALDMEISTER preset, no env overrides
beyond THVM_HEAP_CELLS) PROVES AndAssoc in 11.6s with 372 rules.

  thvm bare-engine: 11.6s, 372 rules                 PROVED
  WM (wmcli):       10.4s, 1673 rules                PROVED (reference)

That is WM-PARITY (12% slower than wmcli, ~5x FEWER rules to crack).
Reproducibility 3/3 PROVED at 60s.  Discovered by reading the WL paclet
test file wl/THVMLink/Tests/atp.wlt:1697 which documents:
  `TFindProof[AndAssoc, WolframAxioms,
              Method -> {"Completion", "CriticalPairWeight" -> "Gt"},
              TimeConstraint -> 400]`  proves at ~706 rules.

THE C BENCH'S `THVM_ATP_WALDMEISTER=1` PRESET WAS ACTIVELY HARMING.
The preset overlays many add-ons over WL's faithful Method->"Waldmeister"
(Mix+51+RHS_IR+UnfailingCP+CP_SET_IR):
  - thvm preset adds: MNF, BWD_SUB, BWD_DEMOD, ORPHAN_MURDER, UNORIENT_INDEX,
    LAZY_NORMALIZE, AUTO_MAXW=30, CH_MaxWeight (mode=1)
  - WL faithful preset stays minimal: Mix + 51 + RHS_IR + UnfailingCP + CP_SET_IR

Both presets FAIL to crack AndAssoc within reasonable wall.  The bare
engine (just GT weight, no preset overrides at all) cracks.

PROBLEM-DEPENDENT TRADE-OFFS:
  - AndAssoc PROVES with engine default (GT), NOT with WL preset, NOT with thvm preset
  - mccune  PROVES with thvm preset (CH_MaxWeight), NOT with engine default
  - wolfram PROVES with NEITHER within 60s

The arc's previous understanding was wrong:  THE 5-COMMIT PRESET-BUILDING
ARC OPTIMIZED FOR THE WRONG TARGET.  Each iteration added a knob and
checked that bench numbers went up on AndAssoc rule-count -- but those
were RULE ACCUMULATION numbers, not CRACKING.  The cracker exists in
the engine default (Gt weight) trajectory, which preset add-ons divert.

PRESET = PORTFOLIO TRADE-OFF, NOT PURE WIN.  Update commit 425b8969
(CH_MaxWeight) to document: it cracks mccune but BREAKS AndAssoc.

NEXT:  expose a "BARE" mode for the bench so the WM-parity AndAssoc
result is easy to reproduce.  And/or build a small portfolio that runs
GT then MaxWeight in sequence so the union cracks {AndAssoc, mccune}.

Full output of the cracking run (THVM_HEAP_CELLS=$((1<<30))):
  steps/sec=242.6  cps/step=31.7  cp-size mean=33.5  ri-retrieval 0.058 cands/query
  trajectory: 372 oriented rules, 84757 live CPs at proof, peak 89267 CPs

Reproduction recipe (no preset overrides):
  THVM_HEAP_CELLS=$((1<<30)) ./bin/test_atp_wolfram_bench andassoc 99999999 60

The 5-commit ATP arc HOLDS for what it set out to do (3.1x rules/30s on
the WALDMEISTER preset, mccune crack), but it MISSED the bigger lever
(the engine default cracks AndAssoc faster than wmcli).  Bench harness
needs a documented "bare engine" path so this finding doesn't get lost.

================================================================
iter 182 -- HONEST: faithful WM port is 14.6x slower per rule
================================================================

CORRECTION to iter 181: the "bare engine cracks AndAssoc in 11.6s" is
NOT WM-parity -- it's a DIFFERENT STRATEGY (engine default GT weight,
KBO, no preset overrides) that happens to crack this specific goal by
a different trajectory.  That is portfolio luck, not faithful port.

FAITHFUL WM ANDASSOC CONFIG (matches waldmeister/andassoc.pr exactly):
  ORDERING: LPO
  PRECEDENCE: p > q > r > nand (skolems higher than function)
  ClasHeuristics: CH_MixWeight (mode=4)
  SelectionRatio: 51
  RHSInterreduce, UnfailingCP, CPSetInterreduce: ON
  (nothing else)

Reproduction:
  THVM_HEAP_CELLS=$((1<<30)) ATP_BENCH_ORD=lpo ATP_BENCH_LPO_SKOLEMS_HIGH=1 \
    THVM_ATP_CP_WEIGHT=4 THVM_ATP_SEL_RATIO=51 \
    THVM_ATP_RHS_IR=1 THVM_ATP_CP_SET_IR=1 \
    ./bin/test_atp_wolfram_bench andassoc 99999999 60

RESULT:
  thvm faithful:  416 rules / 60s, RUNNING (NOT PROVED)
  wmcli native:  1673 rules / 10.35s, PROVED
  rule-rate gap: 14.6x (thvm 7 rules/s vs wmcli 161 rules/s)
  CP-rate gap:   45x  (thvm 10K cps/s vs wmcli 450K cps/s)
  cracking: thvm DOES NOT crack within 60s under faithful preset

CH_Mix formula port (CLAS/ClasHeuristics.c:131): verified identical in
thvm src/atp/_.c case ATP_CP_WEIGHT_MIX.  Math is the same.

PROFILE (faithful port, 60s wall):
  cp-gen      43.13s (70%)  atp_overlap_ij + critical_pairs_pair + cp_visit
  push-norm   39.91s (65%)  atp_cp_trivially_joinable * 632K calls
  interreduce  1.64s (3%)
  unorient-step 51.24s overlaps both phases:
    3.0M calls, 91% wasted, unf-memo 65% hit ratio
    unorientable rules: 286 / 416  (vs 48/372 with KBO default)
  CPs processed total: 613K (vs wmcli's 4.57M)

KEY DIFFERENCE FOUND BY CONFIRMED-WORKING wmcli RUN:
  wmcli derives rule R#1672 = nand(nand(nand(x1,x2),nand(x1,x2)),x3)
    -> nand(x1,nand(x1,nand(x2,x3)))
  after 102 equation-installs + ~4.5M CPs processed in ~10s.
  thvm at 60s has 416 rules + 613K CPs processed -- has NOT
  surfaced the parent rules of R#1672.

UNF_MEMO_BITS sweep under faithful preset (30s wall, bits=15/16/17/18):
  320 / 335 / 336 / 347 rules.  Marginal; the cache is not the dominant
  loss.

What's nominally identical but functionally different:
  - LPO comparison: same algorithm, but thvm produces 68% unorientable
    rules vs wmcli's known ~6% (per wmcli stats: 1673 rules with
    ~100 unorient).  Either thvm's LPO is more conservative on
    Sheffer-style terms OR the precedence-application differs.
  - Discrim tree retrieval: same shape, but thvm's per-query overhead
    is ~17us, wmcli's is sub-microsecond.

NEXT REAL WORK (multi-day, NOT single-iteration):
  - Compare LPO verdicts: feed wmcli's first ~50 rules to both engines,
    log orientation decisions, find the rule where they diverge.
  - Profile wmcli's per-call cost: if it has a structural-hash cache
    or per-position memo we lack, port it.
  - The 45x CP-rate gap means the actual hot loop is slower; this is
    not a knob, it's a code-path rewrite.

The 5-commit ATP arc + iter-181 bare-engine recipe are NOT WM parity.
They are different-strategy cracking-by-luck plus a beefed-up preset.
Real WM parity = same nominal algorithm + same speed; not achieved.

