================================================================
iter 193 -- LPO call-count anatomy + tried levers (no commit)
================================================================

Confirmed stable baseline at 93s/455 rules (after make rebuild caught
the reverted LPO_MEMO_BITS=16 -- a stale 18-bit binary from a sweep
was masquerading as a 180s+ baseline).  Per-rule throughput gap:

  thvm:  93.4s / 455 rules = 0.20s/rule
  wmcli: 10.35s / 1673 rules = 0.0062s/rule
  gap:   33x slower PER RULE (not just 9x wall)

LPO call attribution (43.2M top, 4.94B rec):
  - Per rule: 94K top-level thvm_lpo calls
  - Per top-level call: 115 recursive flat calls (Sheffer binary tree)
  - 1.69M push-time full-R normalizes (each fires ~100-500 LPO chains)
  - Most LPO calls go to unorient_step (11.5M unorient calls
    -> 30M+ LPO each from order-gating rule fires)

LEVERS TRIED (none committed -- all trajectory-divert):
  - LPO_MEMO_BITS=16 -> 18: build picked up, but 18 bits drifts the
    cracker trajectory off the 455-rule path (640+ rules at 180s, no
    PROVE).  Same workload-dependent memo-locality issue as
    UNF_MEMO_BITS.  Reverted to 16.
  - CP_WEIGHT MAX (=1) instead of MIX (=4): 607 rules at 200s, no
    crack.  MIX is the right weight for AndAssoc.
  - LAZY_NORM=1 (push-time normalize gate at WM's 50-symbol
    threshold): 640 rules at 209s.  Eager full normalize at push is
    the cracking trajectory; lazy accumulates wrong CPs.
  - K-D Heap MODULO=50 MODE=MAX (committed infra fc319780, default
    off): 486 rules at 172s.  Activation diverts off cracker.

ANATOMY OF PER-POSITION MEMO BUG (still open, not single-iteration):
  Verifier output (prior iter) caught:
    POS_MEMO UNSOUND at pos=0 phash=0x85a1f030daec3edb ncand=1
    flatsym 66 34 66 2 2  subsz 5 1 3 1 1
    epoch=2 slot epoch=2 rule_index_dirty=0 star=(empty)
  Read of all r_orient assignments (push @6011, shifts @8333/8475/8505)
  shows r_orient on existing rules is invariant -- only ASSIGNED at
  new-rule push or SHIFTED on rule remove (both set dirty=1).
  atp_ri_rebuild bumps both rule_index->n_rules_built and
  unorient_index->n_rules_built; both checked at the dirty gate.

  Hypothesis: the descent's verdict depends on globals NOT in the
  memo key.  g_atp_ri_qsubj = flat[qpos] (the top symbol) and
  g_atp_ri_qend = qpos + subsz[qpos] are set per-call.  For two queries
  with the SAME subtree at DIFFERENT qpos in DIFFERENT subjects, the
  subtree HASH matches but qpos/qend may shift the descent's reachable
  positions.  Need to ADD qpos-relative-data to the memo key OR prove
  the descent verdict is qpos-translation-invariant for hits.

  Carefully: the descent visits positions p, p+1, ..., qend-1.  If the
  subtree at qpos has the same shape, the visit sequence has the same
  topology -- but the absolute positions written into g_atp_ri_star[k]
  differ.  star[] is restored on backtrack so leak is unlikely.  Still
  unsound.  Needs a debug session with both paths active + diff dump.

REAL NEXT TASK (multi-iter):
  Port WM's Stichpunkt schedule.  Today's all-or-nothing cp_set_ir at
  period=16 fires one operation; WM fires DIFFERENT strength bits
  (r/e/s/w/o) at DIFFERENT cadences.  Strength definitions in
  KPVerwaltung.c:130 strengthArray[16] = "o", "r", "e", "re", ...
  "resw".  Default schedule reverse-engineerable from wmcli's verbose
  output ("INTERMEDIATE CP-SET-REDUCTION").  Likely cheap-O orphan-
  murder runs more often than expensive-W reweights.

This iteration: no commit -- gap analysis + 4 negative experiments
+ deeper look at the descent unsoundness.

================================================================
iter 194 -- more negative levers (no commit)
================================================================

  - THVM_ATP_FWD_SUB=1 (forward subsumption at rule add): 142.7s
    cracker (1.5x slower), 437 rules.  Different trajectory -- the
    fwd-subsume drops rules that would have been the cracker seed.
  - THVM_ATP_ORPHAN=1 (orphan murder at pop): 93.2s (identical to
    baseline), trajectory unchanged.  Already a no-op because the
    cracker proves before orphans become an issue.
  - ATP_BENCH_ORD=kbo: 200s+ no crack, 1168 rules.  KBO is the wrong
    ordering for AndAssoc; LPO is correct.
  - LPO_FLAT_MEMO_BITS 14 -> 16: 93.4s (identical), hit rate 36.1%
    -> 36.1%.  The flat-call memo is NOT collision-bound; the misses
    are genuine first-time descents per call.  In-call memo size is
    already fine.

These rule out four more candidate levers.  The per-rule throughput
gap stays at 33x and the structural levers I haven't ported are:
  (a) Per-position negative descent memo (verifier caught real
      unsoundness; root cause hidden in atp_ri_descend_unorient_rec's
      qpos/qend dependence -- not the rule_index state).  Needs a
      proper bisect with both paths active + a per-call diff trace.
  (b) WM's full Stichpunkt schedule for KPMengeInterreduzieren.
      thvm runs strength="re" at period=16 (single op every 16
      rules); WM runs different strength bits at different cadences.
      The orphan-murder ("o" bit) can fire much more often than the
      expensive reweight ("w" bit) -- but on AndAssoc the queue
      never grows orphan-heavy, so this lever may be a no-op too
      (ORPHAN=1 already a no-op).
  (c) AC matching / extended joinability -- WM has GZ_ACVerzichtbar
      that recursively folds AC permutations; thvm's
      atp_cp_perm_subsumed is per-CP at push only, not during
      rewrite.

None is a "single iteration" fix.  (a) is the highest-leverage:
the verifier caught it empirically.  Real bisection requires
running BOTH the memo path AND the live descent path on EVERY call
and recording each disagreement with the full memo state at STORE
time as well as LOOKUP time.

================================================================
iter 195 -- BUG FOUND: missed unf_memo invalidations (committed)
================================================================

Commit 6be88f5f: the unf_memo (whole-subject + new per-position)
invalidated at atp_unorient_step_indexed's rebuild, but TWO OTHER
rebuild sites (atp_rewrite_normalize_indexed and
atp_rewrite_normalize_flatterm_mixed) called atp_ri_rebuild
WITHOUT atp_unf_memo_invalidate.  Both rebuild the unorient_index
too -- so the memo's "no fire" verdicts could outlive their R
context.

Symptom + diagnosis path:
  - Re-applied the per-position no-fire memo with a verifier mode
    (THVM_DEBUG_UNF_POS_MEMO=1) running BOTH paths.
  - Verifier abort: pos=0 phash=0x85a1f030daec3edb ncand=1 -- memo
    said "no fire", live descent finds a candidate; all visible
    state (dirty=0, star empty, folded=0) appears consistent.
  - The (phash, folded) key already accounts for query_folded's
    influence on leaf_collect_unorient.  The missing piece is the
    invalidation at the OTHER rebuild sites.

Effect of the fix:
  - test_atp 135624/135624 -- regression-clean.
  - The AndAssoc canonical 93s/455-rule and mccune 10.9s/214-rule
    cracker baselines were RIDING this bug.  Stale "no fire"
    verdicts elided unorient_steps that should have fired, shrinking
    the rule set the cracker traversed.  With the sound engine the
    same config no longer cracks within 200s; trajectory drifts to
    600+ rules.
  - The previous "9-12x gap to wmcli" claim is therefore overstated:
    thvm's cracker was UNSOUND-fast.  Real gap is at least the new
    >200s wall vs wmcli's 10.35s = >20x.

NEW NEXT TASK (multi-iter):
  Find a cracker config under the SOUND engine.  Likely levers:
  - Re-tune sel_ratio (was 51) -- the FIFO/heap mix may need a
    different anchor under the now-faster-pop trajectory.
  - cp_set_ir_period (was 16): more / less frequent interreduces
    may dovetail with the now-sound rule set.
  - K-D Heap activation (THVM_ATP_W2_MODULO=N THVM_ATP_W2_MODE=1):
    secondary MAX-weight dimension may surface the cracker rule.
  - Goal-directed interleave (THVM_ATP_GOAL_INTERLEAVE) -- pick
    goal-relevant CPs once in K so the cracker rule lands sooner.

The per-position memo itself remains in tree (sound now); profile
its hit rate on a future cracker config to know if it's a win.

================================================================
iter 196 -- WM dokgS port (committed)
================================================================

Commit 79ddf228: push-time rule-subsumption drop -- port of
KPBehandelt's dokgS branch (SS_TermpaarSubsummiertVonGM).

Structural piece WM has that thvm didn't: when a generated CP has
two sides that are simultaneously matched by an existing rule's
(lhs, rhs), the CP is one-step joinable via that rule's instance
and can be dropped without paying the full trivial-join normalize.

Wires existing atp_cp_rule_subsumed (was ATP_CP_DIAG counter-only)
as an opt-in drop via use_rule_subsume_drop / THVM_ATP_RULE_SUB_DROP.

Measured: 121K CPs dropped at sound AndAssoc trajectory of 200s
(out of ~1.2M generated).  Doesn't yet crack the goal in the
200s budget but moves a structural piece into place.  Default
off; pre-existing trajectories byte-identical.

test_atp 135624/135624 -- regression-clean.

REMAINING STRUCTURAL GAPS to full WM faithfulness:
  - Stichpunkt schedule: WM fires different KPMengeInterreduzieren
    strength bits (r/e/s/w/o) at distinct cadences via a sorted
    sample-point list.  thvm runs strength="rew" at period=16.
    Adding per-strength cadence is moderate surgery; orphan-murder
    bit at higher cadence might cull dead-CP load.
  - AC matching during rewrite: WM's TO_/MA_ matchers treat AC
    symbols modulo permutation during ordinary rewriting; thvm
    only handles AC equality at perm-sub time.  Big surgery, only
    relevant when SO_IstACSymbol returns TRUE (NOT for Sheffer nand).
  - SO_IstACSymbol auto-detection: WM derives AC marks from the
    axiom set; thvm requires THVM_ATP_PERM_SUB_MASK env override.

For AndAssoc / Sheffer, nand is NOT marked AC by WM either, so AC
matching during rewrite is NOT the structural diff.  The actual
diff is in the SELECTION trajectory + the missed cracker rule
under sound semantics -- needs a separate per-rule trajectory
comparison (capture WM's first N rules, feed into thvm's selector,
find divergence point).

================================================================
iter 197 -- honest gap diagnosis (push-norm 15.8us/cp)
================================================================

Added per-CP push-normalize diagnostic to bench output.  Profile
under canonical sound config (post-6be88f5f) reveals the dominant
cost:

  phase: pop-norm=0.02s (0%) cp-gen=25.98s (43%)
         push-norm=21.18s (35%) [15.8us/cp] interreduce=15.26s (25%)

Under full structural pipe (perm_sub + dokgS + LRS + BWD_DEMOD):
  phase: cp-gen=49.98s (83%) push-norm=29.86s (50%) [16.3us/cp]

  - 15.8-16.3us per push-time CP normalize.
  - WM's per-CP normalize is ~1-2us (estimated from 162 rules/sec
    throughput x few-CPs-per-rule).
  - The 10x gap is in the NORMALIZE itself, not the surrounding
    bookkeeping; cp-gen, interreduce, etc. add up but per-CP cost
    is bounded by push-norm.

Levers tried this iter (none cracked AndAssoc under sound):
  - THVM_ATP_LRS=1 (Vampire LRS): 711 rules/90s, no crack
  - THVM_ATP_BWD_SUB=1 + BWD_DEMOD=1: 504 rules/90s -- queue drained
    to 1.9K, completion saturates without cracking the goal
    (pruned too aggressively; some needed rule was bwd-subsumed)
  - THVM_ATP_BWD_DEMOD=1 alone: 965 rules/300s, no crack
  - SEL_RATIO sweep (11/21/31/71/101/201): all 400-700 rules at 60s
  - K-D Heap modulo sweep (5/10/20/50): 560-610 rules at 60s
  - LAZY_NORM + full pipe: 676 rules/90s, no crack

The push-norm 10x gap is a real per-CP cost imbalance that no
config-tuning closes.  Closing it needs either:
  (a) Pure speedup of atp_rewrite_normalize (faster discrim-tree
      retrieval, faster apply, cached subterm-normalize results).
  (b) WM-faithful lohntSichBehandlung with a tighter gate that
      lets MORE CPs skip the normalize (LAZY at 50 didn't crack;
      may need different gate strategy).
  (c) Compiled / batched rewrite pipelines (multiple CPs at once
      sharing rule-index scans).

